{"type": "tool_call", "n": 65, "ts": 1790149880.032385, "tool": "submit_memo", "args": {"text": "US ENTITY \u2014 MARCH 2026 OPERATING EXPENSE FLUX vs FEBRUARY 2026\nPrepared by: david.murguia@gmail.com | Date: 2026-04-03 | Currency: USD\n\n=====================================================================\nIMPORTANT LIMITATION \u2014 READ FIRST\n=====================================================================\nI exhausted the session's tool-call budget after pulling the full account-level\nflux and the transaction detail for Hosting & Infrastructure only. The drivers\nfor Marketing Programs and Travel are therefore ASSERTED FROM ACCOUNT ACTIVITY\nBUT NOT YET VOUCHED to source transactions or support. They are flagged below\nas UNVERIFIED and must be substantiated before the controller signs off on the\n\"Flux review\" checklist item. I have not marked that item complete.\n\nI also posted no journal entries and logged no formal exceptions, because the\nbudget was consumed before I could attach support to anything. The completeness\ngaps in Section 3 are raised here in narrative form and still need to be worked.\n\n=====================================================================\n1. TOTAL OPERATING EXPENSE SUMMARY\n=====================================================================\nFeb 2026 total opex:   $261,750.70\nMar 2026 total opex:   $295,463.10\nVariance:              +$33,712.40  (+12.9%)\n\n=====================================================================\n2. LINES BREACHING BOTH THRESHOLDS (>10% AND >$5,000)\n=====================================================================\n\n--- 5000 HOSTING & INFRASTRUCTURE ---\nFeb $31,301.80 -> Mar $40,948.20   |   +$9,646.40  (+30.8%)\nSTATUS: FULLY VOUCHED. Driver holds up and ties to the penny.\n\n  Feb:  JE-00125  2026-02-03  Stackline Cloud, inv STA-12523,\n                              recurring production cloud infra   $31,301.80\n  Mar:  JE-00232  2026-03-03  Stackline Cloud, inv STA-98877,\n                              recurring production cloud infra   $31,148.20\n        JE-00258  2026-03-14  Stackline Cloud, inv SC-M-2203,\n                              ONE-TIME data migration             $9,800.00\n\n  Bridge:  recurring run-rate  -$153.60  (essentially flat, -0.5%)\n           one-time migration  +$9,800.00\n           = +$9,646.40  (exact tie to the GL variance)\n\n  CONCLUSION: The entire increase is a single non-recurring item. Underlying\n  hosting run-rate is flat month over month. April should revert to ~$31.1K\n  absent further one-time work.\n\n  TWO POINTS FOR THE CONTROLLER:\n  (a) CAPITALIZATION QUESTION \u2014 JE-00258 is described as a \"one-time data\n      migration,\" $9,800, expensed in full to 5000. Depending on whether this\n      was migration of our own production data onto new infrastructure versus\n      routine vendor service, some or all may be capitalizable (or, if it is\n      implementation cost in a hosting/CCA arrangement, capitalizable as a\n      prepaid/deferred implementation asset). I did not get to read the\n      invoice. Recommend reviewing DOC support on JE-00258 before close.\n  (b) Both JEs carry support flags (has_support = true), so the documentation\n      exists; it simply needs to be read and the accounting conclusion made.\n\n--- 6400 MARKETING PROGRAMS ---\nFeb $7,898.14 -> Mar $29,686.00   |   +$21,787.86  (+275.9%)\nSTATUS: *** UNVERIFIED \u2014 DRIVER NOT ESTABLISHED ***\n\n  This is the single largest variance in the P&L and I was unable to pull the\n  transaction detail. A near-4x jump in one month is not explainable by\n  run-rate and almost certainly reflects either a campaign/event spend, a\n  prepaid that should be spread, or a coding error. I am NOT going to assert a\n  driver I have not seen.\n\n  REQUIRED FOLLOW-UP before this line is signed off:\n   1. List JEs to account 6400 for 2026-03 and identify items >$5,000.\n   2. Confirm each has support (vendor invoice / campaign SOW).\n   3. Test whether any portion relates to a campaign running beyond March \u2014\n      if so it belongs in Prepaid Expenses (1200), not March expense.\n   4. Confirm nothing was miscoded here from Advertising (6410), which shows\n      $0.00 in BOTH months \u2014 an advertising line at exactly zero for two\n      consecutive months alongside a 276% marketing spike is a coding-error\n      pattern worth ruling out.\n\n--- 6300 TRAVEL ---\nFeb $785.40 -> Mar $7,158.41   |   +$6,373.01  (+811.4%)\nSTATUS: *** UNVERIFIED \u2014 DRIVER NOT ESTABLISHED ***\n\n  Clears both thresholds. February was an unusually low base ($785), so the\n  percentage is inflated and the more meaningful read is the ~$6.4K absolute\n  increase. Plausible explanations (a sales trip, an onsite, conference\n  travel) are all speculation until vouched.\n\n  REQUIRED FOLLOW-UP:\n   1. List JEs to 6300 for 2026-03; identify the large items.\n   2. Confirm expense reports / receipts are attached per policy.\n   3. Note that Training & Conferences (6320) went $542.56 -> $0.00 in March\n      and Meals & Entertainment (6310) went $114.32 -> $49.75. If March travel\n      was conference-related, some spend may be sitting in 6300 that belongs\n      in 6320. Worth a coding check.\n\n=====================================================================\n3. DRIVERS THAT DO NOT HOLD UP \u2014 COMPLETENESS GAPS\n=====================================================================\nYou asked me to tell you if a driver doesn't hold up. The following are not\n\"drivers\" so much as the flux being distorted by March close work that has not\nbeen done. Each of these is still OPEN on the US checklist, and each one means\nMarch expense is UNDERSTATED. The flux above will MOVE once they are posted.\n\n  6800 DEPRECIATION \u2014 Feb $3,425.00 -> Mar $0.00  (-100%)\n    March depreciation has not been recorded. Checklist item \"Record\n    depreciation\" is still open. Below the $5,000 threshold so it does not\n    require explanation on its own, but it is a real understatement of March\n    opex of roughly $3,425 (assuming a flat monthly charge). This is a\n    FAVORABLE-LOOKING variance that is purely a missing entry, not performance.\n\n  6200 SOFTWARE SUBSCRIPTIONS \u2014 Feb $4,139.80 -> Mar $3,339.50  (-$800.30, -19.3%)\n    Breaches the % threshold but not the dollar threshold. However, the\n    checklist item \"Amortize prepaid software\" is still OPEN, so this line is\n    likely incomplete. The apparent decrease may reverse entirely once the\n    amortization entry is posted. Do not treat the -19.3% as a real saving.\n\n  6900 BANK FEES \u2014 Feb $65.00 -> Mar $0.00\n    Immaterial, but zero bank fees in a month with normal cash activity is\n    unlikely. Probably an unposted/uncoded item. Flagging for tidiness only.\n\n  6000 SALARIES & WAGES \u2014 Feb $183,800.38 -> Mar $184,465.91 (+$665.53, +0.4%)\n    Does not breach thresholds. BUT \"Accrue unpaid payroll\" is still OPEN on\n    the checklist. If there is an unaccrued March payroll stub period, this\n    line \u2014 the largest expense in the P&L \u2014 is understated and could become a\n    reportable variance. Same caution applies to 6010 Payroll Taxes\n    (+$50.91, +0.4%).\n\n  AP CUTOFF \u2014 \"AP cutoff review\" is OPEN. Any March-service invoices received\n  in early April are not yet in these numbers. Hosting, Marketing and Travel\n  are all AP-sourced and all could move.\n\n  NET EFFECT: March opex of $295,463.10 should be read as a FLOOR. I would\n  expect it to rise by at least ~$3.4K (depreciation) plus prepaid software\n  amortization plus any payroll accrual and AP cutoff items.\n\n=====================================================================\n4. LINES REVIEWED AND PASSED (no explanation required)\n=====================================================================\n  6100 Rent            $14,500.00 -> $14,500.00    flat, 0.0%\n  6110 Utilities          $919.82 ->    $922.22    +0.3%\n  6600 Office Supplies    $197.75 ->    $281.47    +$83.72, immaterial\n  6310 Meals & Ent.       $114.32 ->     $49.75    immaterial\n  6320 Training           $542.56 ->      $0.00    immaterial (see 6300 note)\n  5100, 5200, 6020, 6030, 6120, 6410, 6500, 6510, 6520, 6550, 6700, 6950,\n  7000, 7100, 7200 \u2014 $0.00 activity in both months, no variance.\n\n  NOTE on the dormant accounts: Employee Benefits (6020) and Insurance (6700)\n  at exactly $0.00 for two consecutive months is unusual for an entity with\n  ~$184K/month of salaries. These may be recorded centrally or in another\n  entity, but if US employees are covered, benefits and insurance expense is\n  missing from this P&L entirely. Worth confirming with the controller \u2014 it\n  would not change the Feb-vs-Mar flux (both zero) but it affects whether this\n  P&L is complete.\n\n=====================================================================\n5. RECOMMENDED NEXT STEPS\n=====================================================================\n  1. Vouch 6400 Marketing Programs and 6300 Travel to source transactions and\n     support. These two lines are $28.2K of the $33.7K total variance and are\n     currently unsubstantiated.\n  2. Resolve the capitalization question on JE-00258 ($9,800 data migration).\n  3. Post the open close entries (depreciation, prepaid software amortization,\n     payroll accrual) and complete AP cutoff, then REFRESH this flux.\n  4. Only then mark \"Flux review\" complete. I have left it OPEN.\n\nI have not marked any checklist item complete and have posted no entries, as I\ncould not attach support within this session.\n"}, "is_error": false, "result": {"status": "memo submitted; task complete"}, "ms": 9}
